% \textcolor{red}{Take some in the Calibration Paper}
The development of trustworthy AI systems increasingly requires robust mechanisms for modeling, quantifying, and interpreting trust, particularly under uncertainty. As AI continues to be deployed in high-risk contexts such as autonomous driving, healthcare, and decision support systems, concerns over robustness, transparency, and reliability have prompted a broad range of methodologies across interpretability, uncertainty quantification, trust propagation, and system evaluation~\cite{framework2021,uqsurvey2020}.

Early approaches to trust in AI often focused on performance metrics such as accuracy or calibration, which implicitly assumed that reliable outputs equated to trustworthiness. Over time, this view expanded to include broader concerns such as robustness, interpretability, fairness, and explainability. Several works have attempted to formalize these dimensions, outlining criteria that an AI system must satisfy to be deemed trustworthy~\cite{framework2021}. This includes robustness to input perturbations, resistance to adversarial examples, fairness across demographic groups, and explainability of decisions. However, these frameworks often rely on static evaluation and do not model trust as a dynamic or propagating quantity.

To better operationalize trust assessment, researchers have introduced a variety of interpretable trust metrics. Notably, the Question-Answer Trust, Trust Spectrum, Trust Density, and NetTrustScore metrics quantify a model's reliability based on confidence levels and consistency across structured queries~\cite{wong2021reallytrustyousimple}. These approaches adopt a black-box perspective and typically perform post hoc evaluation without needing the internal architecture and process. While valuable for visualization and auditing, they offer limited support for compositional reasoning or inference-level trust assessment.

A key advancement in trust modeling has been the incorporation of uncertainty quantification. Modern machine learning systems frequently operate in settings with noisy data, ambiguous labels, or distributional shifts. As a result, trust cannot be decoupled from uncertainty. Several taxonomies have been proposed to distinguish between epistemic uncertainty (model-based) and aleatoric uncertainty (data-based), both of which play a role in interpreting AI behavior \cite{uqsurvey2020}. A broad set of tools has been developed to estimate predictive uncertainty, including Monte Carlo dropout, temperature scaling, Bayesian neural networks, and ensemble methods.

More recently, evidential deep learning has emerged as a dynamic alternative to frequentist or sampling-based approaches. By treating network outputs as parameters of a Dirichlet distribution, these models represent predictions as subjective opinions over the label space \cite{evidentialdl2020}. This formulation enables both point estimates and measures of uncertainty, improving robustness against adversarial perturbations and out-of-distribution queries. However, most evidential methods focus exclusively on the output layer and do not incorporate uncertainty from feature inputs or structural priors.

Conformal prediction frameworks such as PUNCC have also been proposed to offer formal guarantees on prediction sets \cite{puncc2024}. These approaches deliver high-probability bounds on correctness and support runtime decision calibration. While effective in high-risk applications, they do not model trust as a layered or feature-aware signal and often rely on retraining or calibration datasets.

Beyond empirical techniques, several mathematical theories have been used to encode and manipulate uncertainty. Fuzzy logic provides a framework for reasoning with imprecise values, using membership functions to define degrees of truth. It has been widely applied in control systems and rule-based decision-making but lacks a probabilistic interpretation and does not provide mechanisms for belief updating or conflict resolution \cite{framework2021}.

Dempster--Shafer Theory (DST), in contrast, represents belief over subsets of hypotheses and enables reasoning under partial ignorance. It introduces belief and plausibility functions, supporting non-additive probabilities and flexible modeling of ignorance. Despite its expressiveness, DST is known to suffer from computational intractability and ambiguities when handling conflicting evidence \cite{framework2021}.

Subjective Logic (SL) builds upon DST by introducing a computationally efficient calculus of belief, disbelief, uncertainty, and base rates. Opinions in SL are modeled as triplets $(b, d, u)$ with base rate $a$, capturing not only what is known but also how confidently it is known. SL supports several operators for trust reasoning, including consensus (fusion of independent sources), discounting (transitive trust), and recommendation (chained inference) \cite{josang2016subjective}. Unlike fuzzy logic, SL retains a probabilistic interpretation; unlike DST, it avoids set-based combinatorics through closed-form algebra over Dirichlet distributions.

SL is particularly well-suited for trust reasoning because it allows flexible, interpretable, and mathematically grounded representations of incomplete or conflicting information \cite{josang2016subjective}. Its operators support both local and global trust propagation, enabling the construction of layered trust models. These features make SL attractive for applications in reputation systems, sensor fusion, social networks, and explainable AI.

SL has been previously integrated into neural network architectures in frameworks such as DeepTrust \cite{deeptrust2022}. In this approach, each neuron or layer is treated as a node that processes trust, with weights acting as discount factors. Trust is propagated from input features through intermediate layers to the final output, enabling a layerwise analysis of reliability. DeepTrust provides insights into how trust is accumulated or degraded during inference and supports visualization of trust bottlenecks. However, it remains an internal diagnostic tool, and is closely tied to model architecture and training processes.

Other methods focus on output calibration \cite{guo2017calibrationmodernneuralnetworks}. For example, temperature scaling and trust calibration layers aim to correct overconfident predictions, especially in deep classifiers. While helpful for downstream confidence scoring, these methods typically require labeled validation sets and do not generalize well to open-world or continual learning scenarios.

Recent governance-oriented frameworks such as the FRIES Trust Score and PUNCC reflect a growing consensus that trust must be monitored across the full AI development and deployment cycle \cite{fries2024,puncc2024}. These initiatives define trustworthiness through system-level properties such as fairness, safety, robustness, and explainability, often providing certification-style scoring mechanisms. However, they remain coarse-grained and typically operate at the lifecycle level, rather than offering fine-grained, instance-level or dynamic trust evaluation. Moreover, they rarely incorporate symbolic reasoning over trust or account for partial, vacuous, or conflicting knowledge during inference.

Despite these advances, relatively few approaches aim to decouple trust assessment from the AI model itself while supporting compositional uncertainty modeling \cite{framework2021,uqsurvey2020}. Many existing techniques rely on internal model access, retraining, or calibration datasets, which may not be available in practice. Additionally, current uncertainty quantification methods often focus solely on the output prediction, without integrating trust signals from input features or prior assessments. There remains a clear need for approaches that can reason about trust externally, integrate prior uncertainty in a formal framework, and support trust-aware inference across variable contexts. This motivates the development of more flexible and interpretable trust systems that can operate in parallel with AI models, support subjective knowledge integration, and offer formal guarantees under vacuous or degraded input conditions.
